WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.894
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.894 --> 00:00:10.560
One of the most common themes
in my work as an executive

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.475
coach is what I'd call essentially,
people problems.

00:00:14.475 --> 00:00:16.852
Almost all my clients are CEOs,

00:00:16.852 --> 00:00:21.440
the vast majority of them
are leaders of some group of people.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.416
And the dilemma that they face,
if they want to work with me on this,

00:00:25.416 --> 00:00:28.476
is the difficulties that
they're experiencing.

00:00:28.476 --> 00:00:32.865
In some cases with the people they lead
and manage, in other cases with their

00:00:32.865 --> 00:00:37.750
peers, in other cases with investors or
other stakeholders or authority figures.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:38.830
And in that work,

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:43.887
I'm reminded of a great line from the
eminent French film director, Jean Renoir.

00:00:43.887 --> 00:00:48.504
And he says, the hell of life is
that everyone has their reasons.

00:00:48.504 --> 00:00:53.715
And that's really important to remind
ourselves that when we are met with

00:00:53.715 --> 00:00:58.928
somebody whose behavior we find
frustrating or maddening or confusing,

00:00:58.928 --> 00:01:04.409
we often lead to some assumptions,
we assume that they lack understanding.

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:10.373
We assume that that they're acting

00:01:10.373 --> 00:01:15.750
out of spider capriciousness.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.570
We make a whole host of assumptions,
which often justify our own perspective.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:26.441
And are typically very unempathetic, and
what we fail to do habitually is stop and

00:01:26.441 --> 00:01:29.946
ask ourselves,
what is that person's reason?

00:01:29.946 --> 00:01:32.332
I find their behavior maddening or
frustrating,

00:01:32.332 --> 00:01:34.272
they're getting in the way at somehow.

00:01:34.272 --> 00:01:38.682
And what we need to do is slow
ourselves down and remind ourselves,

00:01:38.682 --> 00:01:40.663
everyone has their reasons.

00:01:40.663 --> 00:01:44.312
Very rarely in life, do we encounter
an actual social [INAUDIBLE],

00:01:44.312 --> 00:01:49.005
they're very rarely doing out or somebody
who's deliberately going out of their way

00:01:49.005 --> 00:01:51.892
to screw things up and
make things difficult for us.

00:01:51.892 --> 00:01:55.322
It's much more often the case,
almost universally the case,

00:01:55.322 --> 00:01:59.724
that would we find ourselves in conflict,
it's about misunderstandings, and

00:01:59.724 --> 00:02:02.650
missed expectations, and
incorrect assumptions.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.784
And so much of my work entails
encouraging people to slow down,

00:02:06.784 --> 00:02:11.464
examine the assumptions that they're
making about the other person,

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:15.687
develop a theory of mind,
what is governing this other person?

00:02:15.687 --> 00:02:17.863
What are their beliefs and assumptions?

00:02:17.863 --> 00:02:19.161
What are their goals?

00:02:19.161 --> 00:02:23.010
And can I allow that information
to inform how I approach them?

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:27.839
So rather than assuming,
they're taking these steps and

00:02:27.839 --> 00:02:31.593
actions to kind of mess me up and
get in my way.

00:02:31.593 --> 00:02:35.854
Can I take a broader perspective
on what might be driving them?

00:02:35.854 --> 00:02:39.890
What impulses or reasoning might
be informing their behavior?

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:44.140
It's also important to bear in mind that
in some sense, all behavior is adaptive.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:48.043
And so when we run into behavior that
we find frustrating or confusing,

00:02:48.043 --> 00:02:51.893
it's important to ask ourselves,
well, what are they adapting to?

00:02:51.893 --> 00:02:56.613
And for a leader, it's especially
important to ask, are they adapting to me?

00:02:56.613 --> 00:03:01.213
Am I in some sense creating a condition
that they're finding the need to adapt to

00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:03.078
that I might find frustrating?

00:03:03.078 --> 00:03:09.710
So is there something I can do
to change how I'm showing up and

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:17.264
influence the environment in which
this other person is operating?

00:03:17.264 --> 00:03:18.533
[MUSIC]

